[{"id":"502ec00d7459f2d9","type":"paper","title":"Toroidal mode number transition of the edge localized modes in the KSTAR plasmas","abstract":"Abstract Rapid transitions of the toroidal mode number ( ) have been often detected during the evolution of the edge localized modes (ELMs) in the inter-ELM-crash periods of the KSTAR tokamak plasmas. The mode number transitions accompany changes of the mode frequency ( ) (e.g. changed from 8 to 5 while changed from ~32 to 9 kHz). The observed transition phenomena have been diverse including small and large increase (or decrease) in the mode number and multiple transitions during a single inter-ELM-crash period. Two classes of the mode transitions were identified: non-overlapping transition and overlapping transition. The former case is characterized by the absence of coherent filamentary structure during the transition and the latter case is characterized by co-existence of two coherent filamentary structures with different mode numbers. Each transition process typically lasts a few hundreds to thousands of μ s.","abstract_status":"full","abstract_source":"Crossref","authors":["J.E. Lee","G.S. Yun","M. Kim","J. Lee","W. Lee","H.K. Park","C.W. Domier","N.C. Luhmann","W.H. Ko"],"published":"2015-10-16","updated":"2015-10-16","source":"Nuclear Fusion","source_short":"NF","source_type":"journal","doi":"10.1088/0029-5515/55/11/113035","arxiv_id":"","url":"https://doi.org/10.1088/0029-5515/55/11/113035","pdf_url":"","categories":["fusion"],"tags":["nuclear fusion","tokamak"],"primary_topic":"fusion","methods":[],"classification_confidence":0.64,"classification_evidence":["nuclear fusion","tokamak"],"importance":46,"score_reasons":["来源权重 +40","核物理研究侧重 +4","完整摘要 +2"],"open_access":null,"license":"","publisher_licenses":["https://iopscience.iop.org/page/copyright","https://iopscience.iop.org/info/page/text-and-data-mining"],"rights_status":"unknown","figures":[],"figure_status":"rights_unknown","citation_authors":["Lee, J.E.","Yun, G.S.","Kim, M.","Lee, J.","Lee, W.","Park, H.K.","Domier, C.W.","Luhmann, N.C.","Ko, W.H."],"journal_abbrev":"Nucl. Fusion","volume":"55","issue":"11","pages":"113035","numpages":"","publisher":"IOP Publishing","publisher_url":"https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.1088/0029-5515/55/11/113035","citation_metadata_checked":true,"first_seen":"2026-09-05T23:33:57+00:00","last_seen":"2026-09-05T23:33:57+00:00","history_month":"2015-09","abstract_checked_sources":[]},{"id":"9579862968ad6bb2","type":"paper","title":"Modeling of fast neutral-beam-generated ions and rotation effects on RWM stability in DIII-D plasmas","abstract":"Abstract Validation results for the MARS-K (Liu et al 2008 Phys. Plasmas 15 112503) code for DIII-D equilibria, predict that the absence of fast Neutral Beam (NB) generated ions leads to a plasma response ~40–60% higher than in NB-sustained H-mode plasmas when the no-wall β N limit is reached. In a β N scan, the MARS-K model with thermal and fast-ions, reproduces the experimental measurements above the no-wall limit, except at the highest β N where the phase of the plasma response is overestimated. The dependencies extrapolate unfavourably to machines such as ITER with smaller fast ion fractions since elevated responses in the absence of fast ions indicate the potential onset of a resistive wall mode (RWM). The model was also tested for the effects of rotation at high β N , and recovers the measured response even when fast-ions are neglected, reversing the effect found in lower β N cases, but consistent with the higher β N results above the no-wall limit. The agreement in the response amplitude and phase for the rotation scan is not as good, and additional work will be needed to reproduce the experimental trends. In the case of current-driven instabilities, the magnetohydrodynamic spectroscopy system used to measure the plasma response reacts differently from that for pressure driven instabilities: the response amplitude remains low up to ~93% of the current limit, showing an abrupt increase only in the last ~5% of the current ramp. This makes it much less effective as a diagnostic for the approach to an ideal limit. However, the mode structure of the current driven RWM extends radially inwards, consistent with that in the pressure driven case for plasmas with q edge ~2. This suggests that previously developed RWM feedback techniques together with the additional optimizations that enabled q edge ~2 operation, can be applied to control of both current-driven and pressure-driven modes at high β N .","abstract_status":"full","abstract_source":"Crossref","authors":["F. Turco","A. D. Turnbull","J. M. Hanson","G. A. Navratil"],"published":"2015-10-15","updated":"2015-10-15","source":"Nuclear Fusion","source_short":"NF","source_type":"journal","doi":"10.1088/0029-5515/55/11/113034","arxiv_id":"","url":"https://doi.org/10.1088/0029-5515/55/11/113034","pdf_url":"","categories":["fusion"],"tags":["nuclear fusion","ITER"],"primary_topic":"fusion","methods":["experimental"],"classification_confidence":0.64,"classification_evidence":["nuclear fusion","ITER"],"importance":46,"score_reasons":["来源权重 +40","核物理研究侧重 +4","完整摘要 +2"],"open_access":null,"license":"","publisher_licenses":["https://iopscience.iop.org/page/copyright","https://iopscience.iop.org/info/page/text-and-data-mining"],"rights_status":"unknown","figures":[],"figure_status":"rights_unknown","citation_authors":["Turco, F.","Turnbull, A. D.","Hanson, J. M.","Navratil, G. A."],"journal_abbrev":"Nucl. Fusion","volume":"55","issue":"11","pages":"113034","numpages":"","publisher":"IOP Publishing","publisher_url":"https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.1088/0029-5515/55/11/113034","citation_metadata_checked":true,"first_seen":"2026-09-05T23:33:57+00:00","last_seen":"2026-09-05T23:33:57+00:00","history_month":"2015-09","abstract_checked_sources":[]},{"id":"de7802e40e6c9f3d","type":"paper","title":"First dust study in JET with the ITER-like wall: sampling, analysis and classification","abstract":"Abstract Results of the first dust survey in JET with the ITER-Like Wall (JET-ILW) are presented. The sampling was performed using adhesive stickers from the divertor tiles where the greatest material deposition was detected after the first JET-ILW campaign in 2011 – 2012. The emphasis was especially on sampling and analysis of metal particles (Be and W) with the aim to determine the composition, size, surface topography and internal dust structure using a large set of methods: high-resolution scanning and transmission electron microscopy, focused ion beam, electron diffraction and also wavelength and energy dispersive x-ray spectroscopy. The most important was the identification of beryllium dust both in the form of flakes and droplets with dimensions in the micrometer range. Tungsten, molybdenum, inconel constituents were identified along with many impurity species. The particles are categorised and the origin of the various constituents discussed.","abstract_status":"full","abstract_source":"Crossref","authors":["A. Baron-Wiechec","E. Fortuna-Zaleśna","J. Grzonka","M. Rubel","A. Widdowson","C. Ayres","J. P. Coad","C. Hardie","K. Heinola","G. F. Matthews"],"published":"2015-10-15","updated":"2015-10-15","source":"Nuclear Fusion","source_short":"NF","source_type":"journal","doi":"10.1088/0029-5515/55/11/113033","arxiv_id":"","url":"https://doi.org/10.1088/0029-5515/55/11/113033","pdf_url":"","categories":["fusion"],"tags":["nuclear fusion","ITER"],"primary_topic":"fusion","methods":["experimental"],"classification_confidence":0.64,"classification_evidence":["nuclear fusion","ITER"],"importance":46,"score_reasons":["来源权重 +40","核物理研究侧重 +4","完整摘要 +2"],"open_access":null,"license":"","publisher_licenses":["https://iopscience.iop.org/page/copyright","https://iopscience.iop.org/info/page/text-and-data-mining"],"rights_status":"unknown","figures":[],"figure_status":"rights_unknown","citation_authors":["Baron-Wiechec, A.","Fortuna-Zaleśna, E.","Grzonka, J.","Rubel, M.","Widdowson, A.","Ayres, C.","Coad, J. P.","Hardie, C.","Heinola, K.","Matthews, G. F."],"journal_abbrev":"Nucl. Fusion","volume":"55","issue":"11","pages":"113033","numpages":"","publisher":"IOP Publishing","publisher_url":"https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.1088/0029-5515/55/11/113033","citation_metadata_checked":true,"first_seen":"2026-09-05T23:33:57+00:00","last_seen":"2026-09-05T23:33:57+00:00","history_month":"2015-09","abstract_checked_sources":[]},{"id":"e9e891ea67abae9b","type":"paper","title":"Pedestal confinement and stability in JET-ILW ELMy H-modes","abstract":"Abstract New experiments in 2013–2014 have investigated the physics responsible for the decrease in H-mode pedestal confinement observed in the initial phase of JET-ILW operation (2012 Experimental Campaigns). The effects of plasma triangularity, global beta and neutrals on pedestal confinement and stability have been investigated systematically. The stability of JET-ILW pedestals is analysed in the framework of the peeling–ballooning model and the model assumptions of the pedestal predictive code EPED. Low D neutrals content in the plasma, achieved either by low D 2 gas injection rates or by divertor configurations with optimum pumping, and high beta are necessary conditions for good pedestal (and core) performance. In such conditions the pedestal stability is consistent with the peeling–ballooning paradigm. Moderate to high D 2 gas rates, required for W control and stable H-mode operation with the ILW, lead to increased D neutrals content in the plasma and additional physics in the pedestal models may be required to explain the onset of the ELM instability. The changes in H-mode performance associated with the change in JET wall composition from C to Be/W point to D neutrals and low- Z impurities playing a role in pedestal stability, elements which are not currently included in pedestal models. These aspects need to be addressed in order to progress towards full predictive capability of the pedestal height.","abstract_status":"full","abstract_source":"Crossref","authors":["C.F. Maggi","S. Saarelma","F.J. Casson","C. Challis","E. de la Luna","L. Frassinetti","C. Giroud","E. Joffrin","J. Simpson","M. Beurskens","I. Chapman","J. Hobirk","M. Leyland","P. Lomas","C. Lowry","I. Nunes","F. Rimini","A.C.C. Sips","H. Urano"],"published":"2015-10-08","updated":"2015-10-08","source":"Nuclear Fusion","source_short":"NF","source_type":"journal","doi":"10.1088/0029-5515/55/11/113031","arxiv_id":"","url":"https://doi.org/10.1088/0029-5515/55/11/113031","pdf_url":"","categories":["fusion"],"tags":["nuclear fusion"],"primary_topic":"fusion","methods":[],"classification_confidence":0.56,"classification_evidence":["nuclear fusion"],"importance":46,"score_reasons":["来源权重 +40","核物理研究侧重 +4","完整摘要 +2"],"open_access":null,"license":"","publisher_licenses":["https://iopscience.iop.org/page/copyright","https://iopscience.iop.org/info/page/text-and-data-mining"],"rights_status":"unknown","figures":[],"figure_status":"rights_unknown","citation_authors":["Maggi, C.F.","Saarelma, S.","Casson, F.J.","Challis, C.","de la Luna, E.","Frassinetti, L.","Giroud, C.","Joffrin, E.","Simpson, J.","Beurskens, M.","Chapman, I.","Hobirk, J.","Leyland, M.","Lomas, P.","Lowry, C.","Nunes, I.","Rimini, F.","Sips, A.C.C.","Urano, H."],"journal_abbrev":"Nucl. Fusion","volume":"55","issue":"11","pages":"113031","numpages":"","publisher":"IOP Publishing","publisher_url":"https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.1088/0029-5515/55/11/113031","citation_metadata_checked":true,"first_seen":"2026-09-05T23:33:57+00:00","last_seen":"2026-09-05T23:33:57+00:00","history_month":"2015-09","abstract_checked_sources":[]},{"id":"6d64c0a632e42c31","type":"paper","title":"General approach to the problem of disruption forces in tokamaks","abstract":"Abstract An approach for calculating the force on the vessel wall during plasma disruptions in tokamaks is proposed. It is mainly based on the Maxwell equations and, therefore, is general. Another essential element is the integral force balance on the plasma that strongly constrains the possible solutions. The derived expressions are valid at any disruption scenario and finally give the net forces in terms of the magnetic perturbations behind the wall. The result can be used with magnetic measurements alone. It shows that the geometrical inhomogeneity of the wall and its resistivity are the key factors determining the direction and amplitude of the force.","abstract_status":"full","abstract_source":"Crossref","authors":["V.D. Pustovitov"],"published":"2015-10-08","updated":"2015-10-08","source":"Nuclear Fusion","source_short":"NF","source_type":"journal","doi":"10.1088/0029-5515/55/11/113032","arxiv_id":"","url":"https://doi.org/10.1088/0029-5515/55/11/113032","pdf_url":"","categories":["fusion"],"tags":["nuclear fusion"],"primary_topic":"fusion","methods":[],"classification_confidence":0.56,"classification_evidence":["nuclear fusion"],"importance":46,"score_reasons":["来源权重 +40","核物理研究侧重 +4","完整摘要 +2"],"open_access":null,"license":"","publisher_licenses":["https://iopscience.iop.org/page/copyright","https://iopscience.iop.org/info/page/text-and-data-mining"],"rights_status":"unknown","figures":[],"figure_status":"rights_unknown","citation_authors":["Pustovitov, V.D."],"journal_abbrev":"Nucl. Fusion","volume":"55","issue":"11","pages":"113032","numpages":"","publisher":"IOP Publishing","publisher_url":"https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.1088/0029-5515/55/11/113032","citation_metadata_checked":true,"first_seen":"2026-09-05T23:33:57+00:00","last_seen":"2026-09-05T23:33:57+00:00","history_month":"2015-09","abstract_checked_sources":[]},{"id":"1b60c6810a7952a6","type":"paper","title":"Nonlinear fluid simulation of particle and heat fluxes during burst of ELMs on DIII-D with BOUT++ code","abstract":"","abstract_status":"missing","abstract_source":"","authors":["T. Y. Xia","X. Q. Xu"],"published":"2015-10-07","updated":